I'll explain the problem with Active Record models that embed business rules in ORM lifecycle hooks, then show you how to refactor them using Clean Architecture principles.

## The Problem: Active Record with Embedded Business Rules

Here's an example of what we're trying to avoid:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Active Record with embedded business rules
class User {
  private _email: string;
  private _age: number;
  private _status: 'active' | 'inactive' | 'suspended';

  constructor(email: string, age: number) {
    this._email = email;
    this._age = age;
    this._status = 'active';
  }

  // ❌ Business logic mixed with persistence concerns
  beforeSave(): void {
    // Business rule: users must be at least 18
    if (this._age < 18) {
      throw new Error('User must be at least 18 years old');
    }

    // Business rule: email must be unique
    if (UserRepository.findByEmail(this._email)) {
      throw new Error('Email already exists');
    }

    // Business rule: status must be valid
    if (!['active', 'inactive', 'suspended'].includes(this._status)) {
      throw new Error('Invalid status');
    }
  }

  // ❌ Persistence concerns mixed with domain logic
  save(): void {
    this.beforeSave();
    UserRepository.save(this);
  }
}
```

## The Solution: Clean Architecture Separation

Here's the refactored approach using Clean Architecture principles:

```typescript
